<!-- JOURNAL PAGE -->
<div id="pg-journal" class="pg">



  <!-- JOURNAL LANDING -->
  <div class="gs-sub-view active" id="journal-landing">
    <div class="journal-inner" style="padding:3rem">
      <!-- INTRO -->
      <div class="journal-intro">
        <p class="journal-lead">Sharing stories about the beloved babies who were lost due to stillbirth, late miscarriage and neonatal death helps to open the door for greater connection, knowledge, awareness, and education.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- ENTRY LIST -->
      <div class="jlist">
        <article class="jentry" data-pillar="film" onclick="window.open('https://youtu.be/K3LmoFEKDIQ','_blank','noopener')">
          <div class="jentry-thumb jentry-thumb-video"><img src="https://img.youtube.com/vi/K3LmoFEKDIQ/mqdefault.jpg" alt="Sounds of Stillness — a short film about stillbirth and grief" loading="lazy"></div>
          <div class="jentry-text">
            <span class="jentry-pillar">Short Film</span>
            <h3 class="jentry-title">Sounds of Stillness</h3>
            <p class="jentry-excerpt">An experimental short documentary about stillbirth and silent grief — released on Mother’s Day, and filmed on Mother’s Day two years earlier.</p>
            <span class="jentry-meta">Short film &middot; YouTube</span>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="jentry" data-pillar="history" onclick="window.open('https://mamoszurnalas.lt/viktorija-isgyvenau-vaiko-gimima-zinodama-kad-jis-jau-mires/','_blank','noopener')">
          <div class="jentry-thumb"><img src="/assets/journal-mamos-zurnalas.jpg" alt="Victoria with her baby — Mamos žurnalas feature" loading="lazy"></div>
          <div class="jentry-text">
            <span class="jentry-pillar">In the Press</span>
            <h3 class="jentry-title">&ldquo;I lived through my child&rsquo;s birth, knowing they had already died&rdquo;</h3>
            <p class="jentry-excerpt">Victoria&rsquo;s own story, featured in the Lithuanian magazine Mamos &#382;urnalas &mdash; losing her daughter Maya, and the path that led her to build MIMATIS.</p>
            <span class="jentry-meta">Mamos &#382;urnalas &middot; In Lithuanian</span>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="jentry" data-pillar="history" onclick="openJournalArticle('cslewis')">
          <div class="jentry-thumb"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 128" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M100 44c-14-7-34-9-50-4v60c16-5 36-3 50 4 14-7 34-9 50-4V40c-16-5-36-3-50 4z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M100 44v60" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4"/><path d="M63 56h25M63 66h21M63 76h25" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" opacity=".45"/><path d="M137 56h-25M137 66h-21M137 76h-25" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" opacity=".45"/></svg></div>
          <div class="jentry-text">
            <span class="jentry-pillar">History &amp; Literature</span>
            <h3 class="jentry-title">&ldquo;No one ever told me grief felt so like <em>fear.&rdquo;</em></h3>
            <p class="jentry-excerpt">After losing his wife Joy, C.S. Lewis wrote one of the most honest accounts of grief ever published. What he described mirrors what bereaved parents feel today.</p>
            <span class="jentry-meta">C.S. Lewis &middot; A Grief Observed</span>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="jentry" data-pillar="history" onclick="openJournalArticle('darwin')">
          <div class="jentry-thumb"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 128" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M100 102V42" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M100 66c2-13 12-22 25-24-1 13-11 23-25 24z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M100 80c-2-13-12-22-25-24 1 13 11 23 25 24z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linejoin="round"/><circle cx="100" cy="38" r="4.5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/></svg></div>
          <div class="jentry-text">
            <span class="jentry-pillar">Stories of Meaning</span>
            <h3 class="jentry-title">Charles Darwin lost his daughter Annie at <em>ten years old</em></h3>
            <p class="jentry-excerpt">What his private letters reveal about grief, love, and the limits of reason &mdash; and how loss shaped one of history&rsquo;s greatest minds.</p>
            <span class="jentry-meta">Charles Darwin &middot; 1851</span>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="jentry" data-pillar="art" onclick="openJournalArticle('memoryboxes')">
          <div class="jentry-thumb"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 128" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="68" y="62" width="64" height="42" rx="3" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2"/><path d="M62 62l8-16h60l8 16" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M100 46v58" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" opacity=".35"/><path d="M100 78c-3-4-10-3-10 2 0 5 10 9 10 9s10-4 10-9c0-5-7-6-10-2z" fill="currentColor" opacity=".5"/></svg></div>
          <div class="jentry-text">
            <span class="jentry-pillar">Healing Through Art</span>
            <h3 class="jentry-title">Memory boxes &mdash; what to put in them, and <em>why they help</em></h3>
            <p class="jentry-excerpt">A memory box is not about preserving grief. It is about acknowledging that your baby existed and that their existence mattered.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="jentry" data-pillar="nature" onclick="openJournalArticle('water')">
          <div class="jentry-thumb"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 128" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M50 54c8-6 17-6 25 0s17 6 25 0 17-6 25 0 17 6 25 0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round"/><path d="M50 70c8-6 17-6 25 0s17 6 25 0 17-6 25 0 17 6 25 0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" opacity=".7"/><path d="M50 86c8-6 17-6 25 0s17 6 25 0 17-6 25 0 17 6 25 0" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" opacity=".45"/></svg></div>
          <div class="jentry-text">
            <span class="jentry-pillar">Nature &amp; Grief</span>
            <h3 class="jentry-title">Why grieving people seek <em>water</em></h3>
            <p class="jentry-excerpt">Rivers, oceans, lakes. Research on the nervous system is beginning to explain why so many bereaved parents are drawn to water after loss.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        <article class="jentry" data-pillar="research" onclick="openJournalArticle('ptsd')">
          <div class="jentry-thumb"><svg viewBox="0 0 200 128" fill="none" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M100 94S68 75 68 53c0-10 8-17 17-17 7 0 12 4 15 9 3-5 8-9 15-9 9 0 17 7 17 17 0 22-32 41-32 41z" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linejoin="round"/><path d="M72 62h13l5-11 7 20 5-9h24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg></div>
          <div class="jentry-text">
            <span class="jentry-pillar">New Research</span>
            <h3 class="jentry-title">When grief lives in the body &mdash; <em>what the science says</em></h3>
            <p class="jentry-excerpt">Between 30 and 60% of bereaved parents meet clinical criteria for PTSD. New research shows exactly why grief manifests physically.</p>
          </div>
        </article>
